From: Nex package maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] Settings: read the system htoprc from all three UAPI tiers
Source: no upstream commit; written for Nex against htop 3.3.0
Upstream: not submitted. htop reads one system file at SYSCONFDIR/htoprc, so
 a distribution cannot ship a default that a boot or an administrator may
 replace. The change below adds the generic /run and /usr/lib tiers after
 that file, keeps the existing user paths ahead of all three, and names no
 Nex path in the admin tier.

At startup, when HTOPRC is unset and no user file exists at
~/.config/htop/htoprc or the legacy ~/.htoprc, htop reads SYSCONFDIR/htoprc
(the administrator tier) and otherwise falls back to built-in defaults.
There is no transient or vendor tier, so a running system cannot install a
temporary override below /run, and a distribution cannot ship a default
configuration below /usr.

Try /run/htop/htoprc after SYSCONFDIR/htoprc, then /usr/lib/htop/htoprc,
before falling back to built-in defaults. Settings_read() already returns
false on a missing or unreadable file, so each added tier is only tried
when every higher-priority tier is absent: an administrator file still
wins over a runtime file, which still wins over a vendor file. HTOPRC and
the user paths are unaffected and remain tried first.

diff -urN a/Settings.c b/Settings.c
--- a/Settings.c
+++ b/Settings.c
@@ -846,6 +846,12 @@ Settings* Settings_new(unsigned int initialCpuCount, Hashtable* dynamicMeters,
       this->changed = true;
       ok = Settings_read(this, SYSCONFDIR "/htoprc", initialCpuCount);
    }
+   if (!ok) {
+      ok = Settings_read(this, "/run/htop/htoprc", initialCpuCount);
+   }
+   if (!ok) {
+      ok = Settings_read(this, "/usr/lib/htop/htoprc", initialCpuCount);
+   }
    if (!ok) {
       Settings_defaultMeters(this, initialCpuCount);
       Settings_defaultScreens(this);
